## Deploy
{% if deploy_config.adapter == "render" %}
This project is configured for **Render**. To deploy:

1. Push your repo to GitHub.
2. In the [Render dashboard](https://dashboard.render.com), click **New → Blueprint**.
3. Connect your repo — Render reads `render.yaml` automatically and provisions the service.

> See [Render Blueprint docs](https://render.com/docs/blueprint-spec) for details.

### Scaling with Redis (multi-worker)

By default, the generated `Dockerfile` runs with `--workers 1`. To scale beyond a single
worker, add a Redis-backed KV store so that session state is shared across processes:

1. Install the Redis extra: `uv add pywire[redis]`
2. Update `--workers 4` (or more) in the `CMD` line of your `Dockerfile`
3. Update your `render.yaml` to add a KV store:

```yaml
services:
  - type: web
    name: {{ project_name }}
    runtime: docker
    plan: starter  # or higher
    envVars:
      - key: REDIS_URL
        fromService:
          name: {{ project_name }}-redis
          type: keyvalue
          property: connectionString

  - type: keyvalue
    name: {{ project_name }}-redis
    plan: starter
    ipAllowList: []   # internal access only
```

Render provisions the KV instance first and injects `REDIS_URL` into your web service before
it starts. PyWire auto-detects `REDIS_URL` and uses it for session persistence — no code
changes needed. Use `plan: standard` or higher for persistence across restarts.

Without Redis, stick with `--workers 1` — multiple workers would split WebSocket session
state across processes and break reactivity.
