### Single-machine deployment

The generated `Dockerfile` runs with `--workers 1`. This is the safe default because PyWire
holds WebSocket session state in process memory.

### Scaling to multiple machines

To scale horizontally (`fly scale count N`), each machine needs access to shared session
state. Two options:

**Option A — Fly sticky sessions (`fly-replay`):** Fly Proxy can route all requests from a
session to the same machine using a cookie containing `FLY_MACHINE_ID`. Works for light
scaling but has limitations — VPN users and some corporate proxies may break affinity.

**Option B — Redis (recommended for production):** Add an
[Upstash Redis](https://fly.io/docs/reference/redis/) instance so all machines share session
state:

```sh
fly redis create
# Set REDIS_URL on your app
fly secrets set REDIS_URL=<connection-string>
```

Then install the Redis extra (`uv add pywire[redis]`) and increase workers in your
Dockerfile. PyWire auto-detects `REDIS_URL` — no code changes needed.

**Try Option A first** for light horizontal scaling. Prefer Option B for production.
